{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1161140-speciality-successes","id":1161140,"title":"Speciality Successes","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-11-21T06:03:03-08:00","id":1161140,"title":"Speciality Successes","content":"So was just wondering this, when a specialty is had and a 10 is rolled does your group rule that a 1 will cancel the 10 and therefore BOTH successes, or just 1 of them?<br /> <br /> All of our groups STs have always ruled 1s canceled 10s first and always both successes.<br /> <br /> I ask because the knew Onyx Dice Roller, which I assume is using the rules correctly, does not do this.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/987-tuch","name":"Tuch","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar987_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-21T06:43:24-08:00","id":1161152,"title":"","content":"I do it just like the dice app. 1 removes a success, not a dice roll result. Since 10 is not a single success it is not totally negated by a 1.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12710-pazuzuaxelf","name":"PazuzuAxelf","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-21T10:33:29-08:00","id":1161199,"title":"","content":"While different books have phrased it differently over the years (though the double success on a 10 is a newer thing for the WoD), M20's phrasing is less ambiguous that past ones have been:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mage 20, p. 393</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Game-wise, every 1 you roll takes away one success from your roll. Think of this as \u201cthe rule of one.\u201d If your 1s cancel out your successes, you fail; roll three successes, roll three 1s, and you\u2019re left right where you were before.</div> </div> </div> </div>So for M20 at least, your STs are incorrect by the RAW and are making the rule of one harsher than it is intended to be.<br /> <br /> Even if we're going by other books that might have created that habit, there's still something that's not really supported: assuming it eliminates 10s first. If we take V20's phrasing, their general mentality is closer to how the book phrasing it:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>&quot;Vampire 20, p. 259</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">One more basic of a roll is a \u201cbotch.\u201d Whenever one of the dice comes up as a 1, it cancels out a success. Completely. Take the die showing 1 and one of the dice showing a success and set them aside. In this manner, an otherwise successful action may be reduced to failure.</div> </div> </div> </div>Now, I personally have always run these as identical in application despite the difference, but the V20 phrasing does say to pair 1s with successful dice, and remove both. Doing this for double 10s contradicts the clause that it's one 1 that cancels one success, and I interpret this as holdover language from earlier editions without the double 10s rule. The pairing of botch dice and successful dice is meant to be a visual aid, not an absolute rule.<br /> <br /> But I think it is valid to have interpreted the pairing as part of the process, and eliminating a 10 means negating it completely.<br /> <br /> However, the books never say that you negate 10s first (IIRC even older editions didn't do this as a reason to have started doing it, because the old re-roll 10s were clearly written under the assumption that you checked for botches after re-rolling, not before). That's something I'm not sure where it is coming from, and is definitely making things harsher without book support.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}